      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Wealth inequality in the US over the last 40 years has been primarily driven by the decline in the bargaining power of labor relative to capital, fueled by globalization and technological shifts that favor highly skilled workers and corporations. Simultaneously, significant policy changes, including tax cuts for high earners and the deregulation of financial markets, shifted more income and asset accumulation toward the wealthy. Additionally, the erosion of traditional institutions like unions and declining subsidy for lower-real-income workers have prevented widespread wealth redistribution through wages or social safety nets. These converging forces have created a structural environment where asset ownership and corporate profits expand much faster than real wage growth, especially for low-skilled workers.",
